This is not really a serious YANI (I don't have any particular urge
that it be implemented) just for fun. I'm thinking of a new class: a
scientist class, that would sort of be a cross between a medieval
alchemist and the "geeky, absent-minded, mad scientist who wants to
rule the world and looks like Einstein" stereotype. (And before anyone
gets mad at me for making fun of scientists, *I* am a scientist, and
perfectly willing to poke fun at myself too...)

Scientist class

Stats: 
- Extremely high INT
- High DEX  (good in the lab)
- Medium CON  (kind of wimpy)
- Medium WIS  (smart but not necessarily wise)
- Low STR  (again, wimpy)
- Very low CHA  (that says it all :-) )

Possible races: Human, dwarf (in Tolkien's books dwarfs are sort of
the scientists of the time), elf (?)

Possible alignments: L,N,C (depending on race)

Starting inventory:

+1 scalpel (for dissections)
+0 gloves (you always wear gloves in the lab!)
+0 alchemy smock (lab coat)
+0 T-shirt (no fashion sense :-) )
10 random non-magical potions, all identified
10 blank scrolls (for writing down observations)
25% chance of a brass lantern (Bunsen burner)
10% chance of a magic marker (for writing down observations)

Intrinsics:
- searching (L4)
- identify (L7): this new intrinsic allows the scientist to identify
any non-magical item upon picking it up (chance of success depends on
INT, but probably around 30-50%.) The rationale for this is that the
scientist, trained in careful observation, will be able to note
certain characteristics of the object and use these to identify the
object (for example, picking up a weapon: how rusted is it? How sharp
is the edge? etc.) The scientist would not be able to tell anything
about the B/U/C state of the object, or identify magical objects (all
scrolls, rings, wands, spellbooks, and some potions) because he/she
knows nothing about magic. He/she would be able to identify weapons
(type and enchantment), foods (especially different types of eggs!),
non-magical potions (water, acid, oil, booze, fruit juice, sickness,
confusion, hallucination, paralysis, blindness, and perhaps
(extra/full) healing; this last depends on whether people generally
think it operates by magical or non-magical means.)

To offset the enormous advantage that this confers, the scientist
would be restricted in all spells (as belief in magic and a scientific
worldview are basically opposed.) They are also restricted in long
sword and other powerful weapons, on the grounds that they're fairly
wimpy and haven't had any physical training.

Gods:
Lawful: Isaac Newton
Neutral: Archimedes
Chaotic: Albert Einstein (or perhaps Dr. Frankenstein?) 

Artifact: I've got three ideas for this one. 

1) Philosopher's Stone: like the much sought-after medieval legend, it
turns all lesser metal (iron, brass, and silver) to an equivalent
weight in gold coins when (a)pplied. When #invoked, it acts like a
potion of full healing (the Philosopher's Stone was said to grant
eternal youth.)

2) The Book of All Knowledge (sort of a stupid name, but I couldn't
think of a better one): When #invoked, it gives a single piece of
information. The scientist is allowed to choose from a list of the
following:
- map the level
- identify an object
- reveal a portion of the Castle tune
- search for a given symbol on the map (like with a crystal ball)
- provide an oracular major divination

3) Some sort of laser weapon (don't have a good name: sci-fi fans,
help me out here!) Acts as a permanent light source when carried. When
fired or wielded against a monster, shoots out a harmful ray of either
fire, disintegration, striking (like the spell), digging, or bright
light which blinds the monster, depending on what setting the
scientist has the laser on.

So, what do you think? Any suggestions (especially for quest
